        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Alexandria</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">331 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">April 331 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> (the exact date is disputed), on the site of an Egyptian village called Rhakotis; the architect was Dinocrates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at the western edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, between the sea and Lake Mareotis, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a> (the Pharos); the Library and Museion; the Ptolemaic court; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>; the Patriarchate of Alexandria; the Serapeum; the medieval spice trade; the bombardment of 1882</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Alexandria is the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and its principal seaport.</span></div>
