    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Alexander founded the city in 331 BCE and the Ptolemies, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from it from 305 BCE, made it the largest city in the world within about a century, with a Library and Museion and the Pharos lighthouse.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it remained a granary and a centre of Christian theology, but suffered riots, a massacre under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>, a tsunami in 365 CE and the destruction of the Serapeum in 391.</li><li>After the Arab conquest of 641 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>; the city declined under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and revived from about 1810 under Muhammad Ali, until the bombardment of 1882.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a>, reputed to be 138 metres high, was begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> and finished under Ptolemy II at a cost of 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. Earthquakes destroyed it in the 14th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> citadel of 1477 stands on its site.</p></aside>
    </section>

