    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> founds the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">305 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Ptolemy declares himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> and moves his capital from Memphis to Alexandria</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">47 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> is besieged in the city, and the Library faces destruction</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">391</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> orders the destruction of pagan temples and the Serapeum is destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">641</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> take the city after a siege of fourteen months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1365</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> led by Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> take and sack the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1798</span><span class="rf-text">French troops storm the city during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1882</span><span class="rf-text">British naval forces bombard and occupy the city</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-romance.jpg" alt="Alexander Romance" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_romance._Armenian_illuminated_manuscript_of_XIV_century_(Venice,_San_Lazzaro,_424).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Radiocarbon dating of seashell fragments and lead contamination show human activity at the site during the Old Kingdom and again from 1000 to 800 BCE. Ancient sources mention a trading post here in the time of Rameses the Great, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, but it had long been lost by Alexander's arrival. A small Egyptian fishing village, Rhakotis, existed in the vicinity from the 13th century BCE and became the Egyptian quarter of the city. To the east, where Abu Qir Bay now lies, the port cities of Canopus and Heracleion existed from the 7th century BCE; Heracleion was rediscovered underwater in recent years.</p><p>Alexander founded the city in April 331 BCE, though the exact date is disputed, after capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. He wanted a large Greek city on the coast that would bear his name, and chose a site behind the island of Pharos, to be joined to the shore by a causeway so as to make two harbours. It was meant to supersede the older Greek colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> and to link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. The ancient accounts are numerous and varied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> gives one of the more sober, in which Alexander, lacking chalk, marked out the plan with grain; more fanciful myths are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a>. A few months later Alexander left Egypt and never returned. His viceroy Cleomenes continued the work, and Dinocrates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> designed the city on a grid plan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter.jpg" alt="Ptolemy I Soter" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_I_Soter_Louvre_Ma849_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Alexander's death in 323 BCE his general Ptolemy took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and brought the body to Egypt. He first ruled from Memphis; in 305 BCE he declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> and moved the capital to Alexandria. Inheriting the trade of ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and becoming the centre of new commerce between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Arabian and Indian East, the city grew within a generation to be larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. In a century it was the largest city in the world and for some centuries after, second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Heptastadion, a causeway of 1,260 metres joining Pharos to the mainland, and the Lighthouse followed under Ptolemy I, with the Serapeum; the Museion was built under Ptolemy II and the Ptolemaic mausolea under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a>.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 32 CE counted over 180,000 adult male citizens, besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, women, children and slaves. Estimates of the total population range from 216,000 to 500,000, and some give 500,000 to 600,000 under the Roman principate. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> Alexandria again became Egypt's most important commercial port. Goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> came by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, the port of Aydhab and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and Venetians, Genoese, Pisans and Catalans kept funduqs, fortified quarters under a consul, there. After the Portuguese sea route to India of 1498 its trade declined. The Mahmoudiyah Canal, built in 1820, restored its prominence, and by 1850 the city had returned to something like its former glory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander's viceroy Cleomenes ruled the new city until Ptolemy had him executed in 322 or 321 BCE. From 305 BCE the Ptolemies ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from Alexandria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had influence for over a hundred years before the will of Ptolemy Alexander placed the city formally under Roman jurisdiction in 80 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> was in Alexandria in 47 BCE, courting Cleopatra and besieged by her brother and rival. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> followed his example, and after Antony's defeat at Actium Octavian took Egypt as the emperor's personal property, appointing a prefect who answered to him and not to the Senate. The city commanded an important granary of Rome, one of the reasons he placed it under imperial power.</p><p>The Persian king Khosrau II took the city in 619, and the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> recovered it a few years later. In 641 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> took it after a siege of fourteen months; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> held it again for a time from 645, but it fell for good in 646, ending 975 years of Greco-Roman control. A new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, was founded on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Between 814 and 827 Andalusian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> held the city. It was part of the Ayyubid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> states, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ridaniya">Battle of Ridaniya</a> in 1517, and stayed Ottoman until 1798. The French held it until 1801, when the British took it. Muhammad Ali, the Ottoman governor of Egypt, began its rebuilding around 1810. After the British occupation that began in 1882, the army in the coup of 1952 occupied Alexandria and drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a> from Montaza Palace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/septuagint"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septuagint.jpg" alt="Septuagint" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Vaticanus_(1_Esdras_1-55_to_2-5)_(The_S.S._Teacher%27s_Edition-The_Holy_Bible).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early Ptolemies kept apart the three largest groups of the population, Greek, Jewish and Egyptian. At first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> were probably the plurality. Law was based on Greek, especially Attic, law, and non-Greek texts entered the Library only once translated into Greek. The city had the largest Jewish community of the ancient world, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, a Greek translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, was produced there. The ancient city had a Greek royal quarter called the Brucheum, the Egyptian Rhakotis and a Jewish quarter in the northeast; two colonnaded main streets, said to be about 60 metres wide, crossed near the Soma, Alexander's mausoleum.</p><p>The Ptolemies fostered the Library and the Museion into a centre of Hellenistic learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>, Hipparchus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a>, who became head librarian and calculated the Earth's circumference, are associated with the Museion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> listed Alexandria with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> among the learned cities. Alexandria later became a centre of Christian theology and church government: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> came to prominence there, and Athanasius opposed it. In the Islamic period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> wrote of Muslim saints living in the city. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, then other Europeans, moved in. The poet Constantine Cavafy was born there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-m-forster">E. M. Forster</a> (in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>) and Lawrence Durrell (in the Second) worked in the city and wrote about it.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Alexandria&#8221; cites where it covers Libraries, Poetry:</p><ul><li>Edmund Keely, Cavafy's Alexandria (Princeton University Press, 1995)</li><li>Michael Haag, Alexandria: City of Memory (Yale University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/caracalla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caracalla.jpg" alt="Caracalla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Caracalla_-_Museo_Massimo_alle_Terme.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Pol GRANDMONT, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to one account the Library faced destruction during Caesar's siege of 47 BCE. According to another, the Serapeum of the Great Library was destroyed in 391, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> ordered the destruction of pagan temples and Patriarch Theophilus complied, possibly ending the Library; libraries were closed in the same persecution of pagans. A persistent belief holds that the Library and its contents were destroyed in 642 in the Arab invasion. Which of these ended the Library is not settled. The neoplatonist philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypatia">Hypatia</a> was publicly murdered by a Christian mob in this period.</p><p>Philo reports that in 38 CE disturbances between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> began during a visit by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Agrippa I</a> and led to violence and the desecration of synagogues. Riots followed in 66, and large parts of the city were destroyed in the revolt of 115. In 215 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> ordered his troops to kill all youths able to bear arms, after satires against him; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> over 20,000 people were killed. On 21 July 365 a tsunami from an earthquake near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> devastated the city. Earthquakes struck in 956, 1303 and 1323, and destroyed the Lighthouse in the 14th century. In 1365 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> under Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> took the city and sacked it brutally. The bombardment of July 1882 was the first battle of the Anglo-Egyptian War, and large sections were damaged or burned in the fires that followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/obelisk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/obelisk.jpg" alt="Obelisk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louxor_obelisk_Paris_dsc00780.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Monniaux, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander's tomb, closed to the public in the 3rd century CE, has been forgotten; Greek excavators looked for it without success. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> known as Cleopatra's Needles stood by the Caesareum, and were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s Pillar, a Roman column, 30 metres high with its pedestal, still stands. The catacombs of Kom El Shoqafa were found by accident in 1900, and Kom El Deka has revealed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and Roman baths. The modern city stands over the ancient one, so excavation is difficult; since 1992 Franck Goddio's team has explored the sunken palace quarter and the island of Antirhodos, drowned by earthquakes and tsunamis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">The Citadel</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> was built in 1477 on the site of the Lighthouse. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a> and the Greek Orthodox Church of Alexandria both claim the ancient Patriarchate.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept the city within its sphere of influence for 150 years. In July 1954 it was bombed in what became known as the Lavon Affair, and on 26 October 1954 an attempt was made on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>'s life in Mansheya Square. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956 Europeans began to leave, and nationalisations to 1961 drove out nearly all the rest. Alexandria is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s second largest city and principal seaport. It has more than five million people, counted over an area wider than the municipal limits.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/katibim">Kâtibim (Üsküdar'a Gider İken)<span>1850</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> founded Alexandria in 331 BCE, and as the Ptolemaic capital it became the largest city in the world, with a Library and the Pharos lighthouse. It lost the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> after the Arab conquest, declined, and was rebuilt from about 1810.</em></p>

